\paragraph{Detector calibration matters more than accuracy.}
Replacing TF-IDF+LR (ECE\,=\,0.048) with generative perplexity detectors reverses the advantage across three seeds (GPT-2: AUC\,=\,0.91, ECE\,=\,0.131, $\Delta{=}{+}0.098$; N-gram: AUC\,=\,0.73, ECE\,=\,0.069, $\Delta{=}{+}0.129$; Appendix~\ref{app:detector}).
The TF-IDF vs.\ GPT-2 contrast isolates calibration as the differentiator: comparable AUC but $2.7{\times}$ ECE gap; generative detectors assign high $q$-scores to fluent human text, inverting the $\aeff$ signal.

tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{Effective reweighting requires calibrated discriminative signal.}
Replacing TF-IDF+LR with generative perplexity detectors reverses the advantage across three seeds (GPT-2: AUC\,=\,0.91, ECE\,=\,0.131, $\Delta{=}{+}0.098$; N-gram: AUC\,=\,0.73, ECE\,=\,0.069, $\Delta{=}{+}0.129$; Appendix~\ref{app:detector}).
The TF-IDF vs.\ GPT-2 contrast (comparable AUC, $2.7{\times}$ ECE gap) isolates calibration as one necessary condition; N-gram's better ECE (0.069) does not compensate for insufficient AUC (0.73), confirming that both discrimination and calibration are required.

